    {
      "page_title": "Token Storage, CSP & Slow Hashes",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Never Log, Never URL",
          "items": [
            "Strip Authorization and Cookie in request logs",
            "Same for error reporters and APM traces",
            "Leaked logs are a top real-world token source",
            "URLs land in history, access logs and Referer"
          ],
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Password Storage Rules",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Never plaintext, obviously",
            "Never encrypted: reversible, and the key sits on the breached box",
            "Never MD5, SHA-1 or SHA-256: a GPU does billions per second",
            "Common passwords fall in minutes offline"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Hash",
            "GPU guesses",
            "Verdict"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "MD5 / SHA-1",
              "billions/s",
              "**never**"
            ],
            [
              "SHA-256",
              "billions/s",
              "**never**"
            ],
            [
              "bcrypt cost 12",
              "~thousands/s",
              "ok"
            ],
            [
              "argon2id",
              "memory-bound",
              "==default=="
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Deliberately Slow, Plus Salt"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Tune cost so one verification takes 100-300 ms on your hardware",
            "That makes mass offline cracking economically infeasible",
            "Salt: unique random per password, stored with the hash",
            "It kills rainbow tables, same password gives different hashes",
            "Pepper: secret outside the DB (env/HSM), rotatable, optional"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Self-Describing Strings"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "text",
          "lines": [
            "$argon2id$v=19$m=19456,t=2,p=1",
            "  $<salt>$<hash>",
            "$2b$12$<22-char salt><31-char hash>"
          ],
          "note": "parameters travel with every hash"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Algorithm, cost and salt all live in the string",
            "So parameters can differ ==per user==",
            "That is what makes rehash-on-login possible"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Compare In Constant Time",
          "text": "Use the library's verify or compare. Never hash(input) === stored on raw digests: timing leaks the match."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "7. bcrypt"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Token Storage, CSP & Slow Hashes (cont.)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Blowfish-based, battle-tested since 1999",
            "Cost factor is a power of two: cost=12 means 2^12 rounds",
            "Memory-light, about 4 KB, so GPUs still parallelise it well"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "bcrypt Gotchas",
          "items": [
            "Input is silently truncated at 72 bytes",
            "Pre-hash with SHA-256 and base64 it for long passphrases",
            "Null bytes truncate earlier in some implementations"
          ],
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. argon2",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Winner of the 2015 Password Hashing Competition",
            "The current default recommendation for new systems",
            "Memory-hard, so GPU and ASIC attacks lose their advantage"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Variant",
            "Property",
            "Use"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "argon2i",
              "data-independent",
              "side-channel resistant"
            ],
            [
              "argon2d",
              "data-dependent",
              "GPU resistant"
            ],
            [
              "argon2id",
              "hybrid",
              "this one"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Parameters"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "m = memory, 19-64 MiB",
            "t = iterations, 2-3",
            "p = parallelism, 1-4",
            "OWASP baseline: m=19456 KiB, t=2, p=1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Node Example",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "const h = await argon2.hash(pw, {",
            "  type: argon2.argon2id,",
            "  memoryCost: 19456,  // KiB",
            "  timeCost: 2, parallelism: 1 })",
            "await argon2.verify(h, pw)"
          ],
          "note": "verify reads params from the string"
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Acceptable Fallbacks",
          "items": [
            "scrypt: memory-hard, fine if argon2 is unavailable",
            "PBKDF2 only when FIPS forces it",
            "PBKDF2-SHA256 at ≥600k iterations",
            "New systems: argon2id, no debate"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "cont": true
    }
